A||||21||Cliff Pennock||Stay away from AllStarCamera.com||||13||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||11-29-2001||09:19 PM||||I was looking for the Nikon CoolPix 995 Digital Camera and saw on PriceWatch that All Star Camera had it for $625 (which is a <B>very</B> good price. Their website mentioned the Nikon $100 rebate so they actually advertised it for $525. I ordered it online and my first suprise was that they charged $42.95 for UPS Ground from NY to PA! Next, I received an email to call them to confirm my order. On the phone they asked me if I was aware that the $650 was for the bare camera and that <I>some</I> accessories were not included. I asked what these accessories were. The awnser: no batteries, no charger, no cable, no software, no manual, etc. In short, the camera would not work at all without these accessories. They wanted me to pay an extra $100 for these.<P>For them to sell this camera without these accessories, they physically have to remove them from the box. At first they were saying this is the "wholesale" package. But when I mentioned there's no such thing, and that their website actually stated these accessories <B>are</B> included, they changed their story to "an error on the website".<P>I insisted that I wanted the advertised product for the advertised price but they were unwilling to do so. Of course I canceled the order and wrote Nikon about this. Nikon's response was that they had received numerous complaints about this company already (people who had bought cameras only to find out all "accessories" were missing and that they had to purchase them seperately). But since they weren't an official Nikon dealer, there wasn't anything they could do about it. Nikon also told me to call the credit card company to stop any charges from them, because they <B>will</B> charge your card even though you've canceled the order. In short, All Star Camera was considered a fraudulent company by Nikon.<P>I've already filed a compaint against this company at a few places (one of them being the Better Business Bureau) but according to some customer ratings I have read about this company on other sites, it seems they are getting away with this.<P>I can't believe companies like these are getting away with stuff like this. They should be shut down immediately and the owners should be thrown in jail.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 11-29-2001).]||||reg||13
Z||000001||Cheever3000||11-29-2001||10:18 PM||||Try buying a washer or dryer from Sears.  You find out you must also buy a power cord for $5.  What a joke.  You <B>have</B> to have it, so why don't they just include it in the purchase?  *Sheesh*  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I know it's not the same as your camera order, but sort of a similar principle.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||REDHOTT88||11-29-2001||10:48 PM||||Want fries with that?<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed<BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red & shiny all over - with all the options!)</FONT><BR><B>1986 SE 2m6</B> 4 speed<BR>(Coming soon!)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick's Dad||11-29-2001||10:53 PM||||Cliff, be sure that you stop the payments immediately, and lodge a complaint with your credit card company.<P>Companies like these (most in NY State) do this regularly, even on the street (You see the camera in a window for an unbelievable price, then they tell you that's a camera only, but we're out of stock....).<P>Anyway, they will accept returns, but they will whack you with *substantial* restocking fees, which the NY authorities will do nothing about. Nikon (or any other photo company) will also do nothing, as these are gray market goods, meaning that they were not manufactured for USA, and therefore are not covered by any consumer policy offered by Nikon USA, including warranty. These goods were trans shipped from Europe or Asia, and, because Nikon USA did not handle them, are much less expensive for the dealer to purchase. Therefore:<P>These dealers ream you for the cost of goods in the first place, then;<P>they ream you with shipping charges, then;<P>they ream you with the accessories, or;<P>they ream you with restocking fees.<P>Buy USA goods (as long as you intend to keep them in the USA. Gray stuff will be serviced under warranty overseas) only, from a reputable dealer only, preferably (since I used to work at two of them) from a local shop with people who know what they're talking about.<P>Also check the newsgroups (alt.photo.dealers, I think). People are not shy about reporting their trials and tribulations.||||reg||4
Z||000004||AusFiero||11-29-2001||11:22 PM||||A wholesale pack hey. Does Nikon know where they are getting their cameras? Can the put a stop to it somehow? Probably not I suppose but that really sux.<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRiAD||11-29-2001||11:30 PM||||Nikon won't stop whoever's selling to these guys, because they are moving cameras...<P>Wait till you buy an electronic item, that wasn't intended for the US and is the wrong VOLTAGE! Argh!<P>||||reg||1
